{"name":"Mudbrick","hex":"#a46960","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"mudbrick","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Adobe brick; unfired brick made from mud or clay mixed with straw."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/mudbrick"}],"entries":[{"title":"Mudbrick","blocks":[{"text":"A mudbrick, also called an unfired brick, is an air-dried brick composed of a mixture of mud with a binding material, such as rice husks or straw. Mudbricks are known to have been used since 9000 BCE."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6d/Mudbricks_in_Palestine_2011.jpg/330px-Mudbricks_in_Palestine_2011.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mudbrick","wikidata":"Q2090236"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/df/Choghazanbil2.jpg/330px-Choghazanbil2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/df/Choghazanbil2.jpg/330px-Choghazanbil2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Mudbrick was used for the construction of Elamite ziggurats—some of the world's largest and oldest constructions. Choqa Zanbil, a 13th-century BCE ziggurat in Iran, is similarly constructed from clay bricks combined with burnt bricks.[1]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AChoghazanbil2.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2b/Mud-brick_stamped_with_seal_impression_of_raised_relief_of_the_Treasury_of_the_Vizier._From_Lahun%2C_Fayum%2C_Egypt._12th_Dynasty._The_Petrie_Museum_of_Egyptian_Archaeology%2C_London.jpg/500px-thumbnail.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2b/Mud-brick_stamped_with_seal_impression_of_raised_relief_of_the_Treasury_of_the_Vizier._From_Lahun%2C_Fayu